Python can count the matching values directly with `sum`. The condition produces `True` for odd values and `False` for even values, which act like `1` and `0` when added.

```python
class Solution:
    def countOdd(self, arr, n):
        return sum(1 for i in range(n) if arr[i] % 2 != 0)
```

The method only reads the first `n` elements, as required by the parameter. For `arr = [5, 8, 11, 14, 17]` and `n = 5`, the count is `3`.

A value of `n = 0` returns `0`, and no input value is modified.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the generator used while counting

The array can be divided into two smaller parts. The helper counts the left half and right half, then adds their results. This gives a different way to visit every element without changing the array.

```go
func countOdd(arr []int, n int) int {
    var count func(int, int) int

    count = func(left int, right int) int {
        if left >= right {
            return 0
        }

        if right-left == 1 {
            if arr[left]%2 != 0 {
                return 1
            }
            return 0
        }

        middle := left + (right-left)/2
        return count(left, middle) + count(middle, right)
    }

    if n <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return count(0, n)
}
```

For `[3, 6, 10, 13]`, the left part finds `3`, the right part finds `13`, and the answer is `2`. The code assumes `n` does not exceed the length of `arr`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the divide-and-conquer call stack

This version splits the work by using the first element and then solving the rest of the array recursively. An odd first value contributes `1`, while an even value contributes `0`.

```java
class Solution{
    public int countOdd(int[] arr, int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int current = (arr[n - 1] % 2 != 0) ? 1 : 0;
        return current + countOdd(arr, n - 1);
    }
}
```

An empty array gives `0` immediately. The array contents stay unchanged, but recursion uses stack space, so this is less suitable for a very large `n`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive calls

An odd integer has its lowest binary bit set to `1`, so `(value & 1)` can test parity without using division. The loop still visits each element once.

```csharp
class Solution{
    public int countOdd(int[] arr, int n) {
        int oddCount = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if ((arr[i] & 1) == 1) {
                oddCount++;
            }
        }

        return oddCount;
    }
}
```

This works for positive and negative integers because the lowest bit is still different for odd and even values. For example, `[-3, -2, 0, 7]` contains two odd numbers.

The array is read only, and an empty input produces `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A direct scan is enough here. Each value is checked with `% 2`, and the counter increases only for odd values. The input array is not changed.

