## Intuition
An integer is odd if and only if it is not divisible by 2. In binary representation, an odd number always has its least significant bit set to 1. Testing whether each element satisfies the oddness condition independently allows for an accumulation count in a single pass over the collection.

Key Bug Note: In C++, arr[i] % 2 == 1 fails for negative odd integers (e.g., -3 % 2 yields -1). To handle all integers correctly, check arr[i] % 2 != 0 or use the bitwise operation (arr[i] & 1) == 1.

## Approach
Initialize a Counter: Maintain a variable odd initialized to 0 to track the total number of odd elements.

Linear Scan: Iterate through the array from index 0 to n - 1.

Parity Check: For each element arr[i], evaluate whether it is odd using arr[i] % 2 != 0 (or arr[i] & 1). If the condition evaluates to true, increment odd by 1.

Return Total: Once the loop completes, return odd.

Edge Cases Handled:

Empty Array (n = 0): The loop condition i < n evaluates to false immediately, returning 0.

All Even / All Odd Elements: Correctly returns 0 or n, respectively.

Negative Numbers: Using arr[i] % 2 != 0 or bitwise & 1 accounts for negative odd numbers where modulo returns negative values in C++.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
public:
    int countOdd(int arr[], int n){
          int odd=0;
          for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            if(arr[i]%2==1)
             odd=odd+1;
          }
          return odd;
    }
};
```

